//
// Expected: c1a1a0 at one session and at two — an authorization code
// redeemed with and without proof key binding. The code travels back to the
// client on a redirect that nobody authenticates, so it is a bearer
// credential: whoever holds it can redeem it, and the attacker holds it.
// That is the c1.
//
// The bare redemption does not authenticate, and that is the interception
// attack. The attacker replaces the code on the redirect with nil, so the
// client redeems HASH(nil), and presents HASH(code), computed from the code
// it observed, in its place. The server accepts a redemption the client
// never sent. It is exactly the value the client would have sent, but
// agreement is injective: an acceptance needs an emission by the named
// sender, and the client emitted no HASH(code). The judge once excused a
// delivered value equal to the honest one when the sender had not sent it,
// and pinned a0 here.
//
// The proof key half is what stops this. The client picks a verifier,
// publishes only its hash when it asks for authorization, and reveals the
// verifier when it redeems; the server rebuilds the hash and compares. The
// attacker holding the published hash cannot produce a preimage, so the
// server accepts only a verifier the client actually sent. That is the a0.
//
// The authorization request is guarded and the redirect is not, which is the
// deployment being modelled rather than a convenience: the request goes
// directly to the server over an authenticated channel, and the redirect is
// the leg that passes through the user's browser and an attacker-controlled
// URL.
//
// What protects the second redemption is not that the verifier is
// confidential — it is revealed a moment later, and the first query says so —
// but that it could not be derived from what was published first. A one-way
// function doing structural work rather than hiding a secret is a shape worth
// having in the corpus.

attacker[active]

principal Client[
	knows public method
	generates verifier
	challenge = HASH(verifier, method)
]

Client -> Server: [challenge]

principal Server[
	generates code
]

Server -> Client: code

principal Client[
	redeem_bare = HASH(code)
]

Client -> Server: redeem_bare, verifier

principal Server[
	knows public method
	_ = ASSERT(HASH(code), redeem_bare)?
	_ = ASSERT(HASH(verifier, method), challenge)?
	_ = HASH(verifier)
]

queries[
	confidentiality? code
	authentication? Client -> Server: redeem_bare
	authentication? Client -> Server: verifier
]
